{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310820,"nonce":"YQ5wteLyGj/cvabuKKY6SctI7UI=","title":"The wasm \\\"decode regression\\\" was Q8 KV flash kernel scalar-on-wasm; SIMD128'd it +18% (21.5→25.3 tok/s), matching F32 while keeping ...","message":"User reported in-guest wasm decode \"stuck at 20, used to be 24-25 tok/s\" (config:\nRAYZOR_WASM_NO_HOST_MATMUL=1 + RAYZOR_KV_Q8=1 + REQUANT + NO_HOST_FLASH, 5000 tok).\nIt was **NOT a code regression** — proven by: (a) every in-binary A/B neutral\n(host-flash seam bypass, RAYZOR_REQUANT_Q6K drop, pre-55ae003 kernel revert,\nRAYZOR_WASM_FREE=0 reclamation-off — all within noise); (b) wasmtime 46→47 ISLE\ndiff shows 47 ADDED relaxed_dot→AArch64 SDOT (a win); (c) bisect to pre-session\n75471fc TRAPS (OOB in wasm qmatmul) — the in-guest path is YOUNG, only recently\nfunctional, so there was no faster past state to regress from.\n\nROOT CAUSE: the **Q8 KV flash kernel** (`nue-plugins::flash_attn_decode_q8`'s\n`dequant_q8_0_block` / `dot_block_f32` / `axpy_block_f32`) was **scalar on wasm** —\nits fast path was `#[cfg(target_arch=\"aarch64\")]` (NEON), and wasm32 fell to the\n`#[cfg(not(aarch64))]` scalar loop. c1979f7's \"SIMD128 flash dot/axpy\" only\ncovered the **F32** flash kernel (runtime-wasm `flashAttnDecode`), NOT this\nside-module Q8 kernel. So on wasm: F32 KV attention = SIMD128 (38ms/25.5 tok/s),\nQ8 KV attention = scalar (44ms/21.5 tok/s). The user's \"24-25\" was the F32 path;\nswitching to Q8 (for the 3.76× memory saving) silently hit the scalar kernel.\n\nFIX (uncommitted, validated): added `#[cfg(target_arch=\"wasm32\")]` SIMD128\nversions of all three (core::arch::wasm32: v128_load, i16x8/i32x4_extend,\nf32x4_convert/mul/add, splat, extract_lane), narrowed the scalar gate to\n`all(not(aarch64), not(wasm32))`. Mul+add (NOT relaxed_madd) for determinism.\nRebuild the side-module with `nue-plugins/build-wasm-pic.sh` (recovered from\nb49c447^ — the committed script was dropped when rpkg took over packaging; run as\na one-off, +simd128, -Z build-std PIC). RESULT: **Q8 in-guest 21.5→25.3 tok/s\n(+18%), p50 44.75→37.75ms — now matches F32 speed AND keeps Q8 memory savings.**\nFrance→Paris preserved, deterministic (run1==run2). Native dylib unaffected\n(wasm32-gated; aarch64 NEON path untouched). See [[perf_wasm_host_parallel_flash_q8]].\n\nLEVER NOTE: in-guest decode uses only ~330% CPU (matmul ~5 cores, attention +\norchestration serial on 1). Decode also decays with context (O(context)\nattention): ~27 tok/s short → ~21 long — part of why \"24-25\" felt like a\nregression when measured at different lengths. F32 KV is faster but ~3.76× more\nmemory (may OOM at very long context — why the user chose Q8).\n\nFOLLOW-ON WASM-BASELINE CAMPAIGN (user wants to close the ~3.3× native gap;\nnative JIT ~90 tok/s, wasm in-guest ~28). Cumulative this arc 21.5→~28 tok/s\n(+30%), all validated France→Paris + deterministic:\n  - Q8 flash SIMD128 (+18%, above).\n  - In-guest matmul auto threads 6→7 (runtime-wasm/qtensor.rs ~769): 8th P-core\n    absorbs the spin-joining caller; 8 oversubscribes. +10%. RAYZOR_WASM_GUEST_THREADS overrides.\n  - x→Q8_K prequant SIMD128 (runtime-core/quant/q8_k.rs quantize_block_q8_K: was\n    aarch64-only, scalar on wasm — the un-vectorized twin of the dot kernel).\n    wasm32 path uses trunc(x+copysign(0.5,x)) to match roundf ties-away (wasm\n    SIMD has no round-ties-away convert) → bit-identical. Only ~3% (not the\n    bottleneck the lever workflow estimated).\nL1 ATTEMPTED + REVERTED (condvar→spin in-guest pool, ~150 LOC wasm_runner.rs):\nREGRESSED p50 34→44ms, p95 39→66-74ms. The native pool's spin win does NOT\ntransfer to the in-guest wasm path: the serial slice (attention+orchestration on\n1 core) is ~40% here vs tiny natively, so 8 spinning workers STEAL cores from the\nmain thread's serial work (p95 blowup = starved serial steps). Fundamental\ntension: spin (steals cores during serial work) vs park (dispatch becomes a futex\nunpark, no better than condvar). Condvar — which parks workers between jobs — is\nactually the right choice for the in-guest path. France→Paris stayed correct (perf\nregression, not a bug). Don't retry condvar→spin on the in-guest pool. NOTE: the\nhost-flash decouple (in-guest matmul + host-flash attention) has the SAME\noversubscription problem (host pool spins, committed spin→park threshold ~0.5s too\nlong to park during the ~1ms matmul). The clean attention-parallelization is L4:\nband kv_heads on the IN-GUEST CONDVAR pool (parks when idle → no oversubscription)\nwith pre-allocated per-band scratch.\n\nL4 ATTEMPTED + REVERTED (parallel in-guest Q8 flash, ~290 LOC: kernel ported to\nruntime-wasm/qtensor.rs + banded kv_heads across the in-guest condvar pool with\npre-allocated per-band scratch, zero worker malloc; dropped from Q8_SIDE_FNS so\nthe linker resolves the guest call to the main module). CORRECT + DETERMINISTIC\n(France→Paris, 300-tok greedy run1==run2 — the pre-alloc-scratch design beat the\nprior 8b90d1b non-determinism). But NEUTRAL-to-slightly-NEGATIVE everywhere\ntestable: cache 430 (785-tok run, temp 0.48 EOS'd early) p50 34.5 vs serial 33.75\n(~2% slower); cache 1100 42.5 vs 42.25. The condvar spawn/join overhead (7\nspawns+7 joins per attention call) cancels the parallelization at all reachable\ncontext. The +20-35% the lever workflow predicted needs cache \u003e\u003e 1500 to\nmaterialize, but greedy/temp-0.48 generation EOS's ~785 tokens so the user's\nworkload never reaches it. Reverted (don't ship neutral-negative complexity);\nkernel design preserved in git history (commit d6c042e on branch, then reverted)\n+ RAYZOR_WASM_NO_FLASH_PAR toggle. Revisit only with a workload proven to reach\ncache \u003e\u003e 1500. KEY LESSON: both structural levers (L1 spin, L4 parallel-attn)\ndied on the SAME wall — the in-guest path is condvar-dispatch-bound, and you\ncan't remove that cost by spinning (oversubscribes the ~40% serial slice). The\nreal ceiling-breakers (untried, larger): hybrid spin-during-matmul/park-during-\nattention dispatch, or cutting the Haxe orchestration overhead.\n\nRANKED REMAINING LEVERS (workflow wf_e03e4348): L1 condvar→spin in-guest pool\ndispatch (wasm_runner.rs JobSlot Mutex+Condvar + mpsc → spin protocol like\nruntime/worker_pool.rs; +15-25%, ~150 LOC 1 file, RISK garbage-tokens via\nmemory-ordering + oversubscription of the serial slice → needs spin-THEN-park).\nL4 parallel Q8 attention (port side-module kernel to runtime-wasm + band\nkv_heads, pre-alloc scratch zero-worker-malloc — prior revert 8b90d1b root cause\nwas concurrent guest malloc NOT missing TLS; +20-35% AT LONG CONTEXT, best for\nthe user's 5000-tok runs). L3 fused QKV (+5-10%). L5 elementwise/RMSNorm SIMD\n(diffuse). NO wasmtime config freebies (SDOT active, bounds elided, OptLevel::Speed,\n4GB reservation all already set). Validation gate for L1/L4: France→Paris\nbyte-identical + \u003e500-tok gen to EOS + run-to-run determinism (garbage-token, not\ncrash, is the failure mode).\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `perf_wasm_q8_flash_simd128` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}